8. SCAN EXCLUSIONS / exists / MISSING DIR — PASS
================================================================
Ran the P14 tests verbosely, all real:

    --- PASS: TestHandleImportScan_ListsCSVWithDateRange
    --- PASS: TestHandleImportScan_ExcludesNonCSVSubdirSymlink
    --- PASS: TestHandleImportScan_ExistsFlag
    --- PASS: TestHandleImportScan_MissingDirectory
    --- PASS: TestHandleImportScan_EmptyDirectory
    --- PASS: TestHandleImportScan_HonorsConfiguredImportDir
    ok  budget2/internal/handlers/explorer  0.005s   exit=0
    --- PASS: TestImportDir_DefaultUsesXDG
    --- PASS: TestImportDir_DefaultFallsBackToHome
    --- PASS: TestImportDir_EnvOverride
    --- PASS: TestImportDir_LoadHonorsDefault
    ok  budget2/internal/config  0.002s   exit=0

Read the bodies rather than trusting names. The exclusions test writes real.csv,
notes.txt, sub/nested.csv and a link.csv symlink, then asserts exactly 1 entry
named real.csv — that single assertion kills non-CSV, recursion and symlink at
once. ExistsFlag seeds test0.csv into the data dir and asserts Exists=true for
test0.csv and false for new.csv. MissingDirectory points cfg at a non-existent
path and asserts rec.Code == 200, zero entries, non-empty message containing
"not found" — explicitly NOT a 500.

Confirmed against the handler: scanImportDirectory returns (nil, message) for
both the empty-path and os.Stat-error cases and for a Glob error, and
handleImportScan always writes 200 with {ImportEntries, ImportMessage,
ImportPath}. There is no error branch that can produce a 500. The exists set is
built from the package-global `loader`, which is bound to cfg.DataDirectory — the
same source the file-manager page lists from, as specified.

