9. ACCESSIBILITY vs /home/darrell/work/agents2/ACCESSIBILITY.md — PASS
================================================================
Panel at filemanager.html:57-95, row partial `{{define "import-scan"}}` at :1257.
Note A-7/A-12/A-13 are worded for the swarm dashboard (ledger table, verdict
evidence, spend.jsonl) and have no counterpart here; I judged the applicable
points.

A-4 Keyboard. Rescan is a real <button type="button">; every row control is a
native <input type="checkbox">; delete_source is a native checkbox. All natively
focusable in DOM order. The Import button is `disabled`, hence outside the tab
order — that is the correct encoding of the brief's "leave the control inert",
and its state is exposed via aria-disabled="true", not by styling alone.

A-5 Visible focus. Rescan carries `focus:outline-none focus:ring-2
focus:ring-indigo-500` — outline is removed but replaced by an equal-or-better
ring, which is what A-5 requires. The checkboxes add `focus:ring-indigo-500`
WITHOUT focus:outline-none, so they keep the native outline plus a ring. No bare
`outline: none` anywhere in the new markup.

A-9 Never color-only — the point the brief singles out. Each already-present row
renders literal text: `(already present, will be skipped)` at :1272, alongside
`disabled:opacity-60`. The state survives with color and opacity stripped, so a
colorblind operator reads the same thing. PASS.

Label association. Row checkbox `id="import-entry-{{$i}}"` is paired with an
explicit `<label for="import-entry-{{$i}}">` (:1265, :1268); delete_source uses a
wrapping implicit <label> (:81). Both are programmatically associated. The new
row checkbox is actually stricter than the pre-existing file-list checkbox at
:1232, which has no label at all (pre-existing defect, not P14's).

A-10 Live region: `<div id="import-scan-list" aria-live="polite">` wraps the
swap target, so htmx replacements are announced politely. (Minor: aria-busy is
hard-coded "false" and never toggled — harmless.)

A-14 No inline handlers: grep for on(click|change|input|submit)= across the new
panel returns "(none)". Behavior is hx-get only. The onclick/onchange handlers
elsewhere in this file predate P14.

A-15 Target size: checkboxes are w-4 h-4 (16px), under the 24px ideal, but each
sits in a `py-1.5` row (~28px pitch, satisfying 2.5.8's spacing exception) with a
large `flex-1 cursor-pointer` label extending the hit area. This also matches the
file's pre-existing checkbox convention (:1235 is byte-identical in sizing and
was not touched by P14 — the diff hunks are @@ -54,6 +54,45 @@ and
@@ -1214,3 +1253,28 @@). Not a regression.

A-1 axe: NOT RUN. These pages are Go server-rendered and I had no running
instance; I verified the applicable points by markup inspection instead. Stating
this rather than claiming a clean audit I did not perform.

Template validity — the trap here is that all six handler tests run with
renderer == nil and therefore exercise only the JSON fallback, so none of them
touches the new "import-scan" partial. The partial uses `div` and `toFloat`,
which are parse-time FuncMap lookups. Confirmed both are registered
(internal/templates/render.go:83 "div", :85 "toFloat") and confirmed the real
template tree actually parses, since insights/handlers_test.go:622 calls
templates.New(ProjectRoot()+"/web/templates", true) over the whole directory:
    --- PASS: TestHandleInsights_WithRenderer
    --- PASS: TestInsightsVerdictBar_Render
    ok  budget2/internal/handlers/insights  0.139s
A parse error or unknown function in filemanager.html would have failed that.
Page-load render is also safe: HandleFileManagerPage passes
map[string]interface{}, and missing map keys yield nil rather than an error, so
`{{template "import-scan" .}}` renders the "Click Rescan…" empty state.

================================================================
DEFECT FOUND — gofmt regression, reported not blocking
================================================================
This does not map to any of the nine acceptance items and the brief's stated gate
is `go build && go vet && go test && staticcheck` (all green), so I am not
failing on it — but it is a real, objective regression introduced by P14 and the
lead should clear it before merge.

I was told the repo has pre-existing gofmt deviations and to judge only whether
P14 made things worse. I ran the delta: extracted each touched file at HEAD and
compared gofmt diff size against the worktree.

    FILE                                    HEAD    WORKTREE
    internal/config/config.go                  0  ->  12   REGRESSED
    internal/handlers/explorer/handlers.go     0  ->  12   REGRESSED
    internal/handlers/explorer/handlers_test.go 0 ->   9   REGRESSED
    internal/config/config_test.go            25  ->  25   pre-existing, unchanged

Three files that were gofmt-clean at HEAD are now dirty, and all three
deviations sit inside P14's own edits:

    config.go:39      -	ImportDirectory   string `json:"import_directory"`
                      +	ImportDirectory    string `json:"import_directory"`
    handlers.go:518   -		"ActiveTab":        "filemanager",
                      +		"ActiveTab":       "filemanager",
                      (misaligned when the longer "ImportDirectory" key was added)
    handlers_test.go  two trailing blank lines at EOF

Purely cosmetic, no behavioral effect, fixed by
`gofmt -w internal/config/config.go internal/handlers/explorer/handlers.go
internal/handlers/explorer/handlers_test.go`. Flagging it explicitly so it is not
lost; blocking a Tier-2 task and risking a three-judge dispute over three
whitespace characters would be disproportionate when every stated acceptance
criterion is met.

================================================================
CONCLUSION
================================================================
All nine required confirmations verified independently against code and executed
output, not against test names or worker claims. The two highest-risk items —
scope creep via an early write path, and a vacuous symlink test — were both
probed directly (ensureDirectories inspected; symlink creation proven functional
on this host) and both hold. One cosmetic gofmt regression recorded above for the
lead to clear. VERDICT: PASS.
